    37	## Definition
    38	
    39	Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Fix a renormalization scale $\mu_0>0$ and a common open interval $J\subset(0,\infty)$ of renormalization scales $\mu$. Write $t=\log(\mu/\mu_0)$, so $t$ is dimensionless. A threshold datum consists of a high-energy description, denoted $+$, and a lower-energy effective description, denoted $-$. The descriptions have a common declared light-field sector and differ by the degrees of freedom retained across a specified threshold of mass $M>0$. The external-energy domain and the range of validity of the local expansion are supplied separately; in a heavy-particle expansion they ordinarily require external energy $E_{\mathrm{ext}}<M$. The Wilsonian matching scale $\mu_W$, the renormalization scale $\mu$, the threshold mass $M$, the external-energy bound $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, and the cutoff $\Lambda$ have mass dimension one and are distinct data; no equality between $\mu_W$ and $\mu$ is part of this definition.
    40	
    41	For each sign $\sigma\in\{+,-\}$, supply a finite coefficient-coordinate space $\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ with $N_\sigma\ge1$, a declared ordered operator basis and power-counting truncation, and a renormalization-scheme coordinate map. The vector $C_\sigma(t)\in\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ is the column of dimensionless Wilson coefficients in that basis. The corresponding finite-order operator expression uses the supplied convention $C_{\sigma,i}O_{\sigma,i}/\Lambda^{d_i-4}$, with each $O_{\sigma,i}$ of engineering dimension $d_i$. Write $\rho_{N_\sigma}$ for the realification that lists the real and imaginary parts of each coefficient. A supplied RG vector field is a continuous map $\beta_\sigma$ on an open subset of the realified scale-state space; an RG trajectory satisfies
    42	$$\frac{d}{dt}\rho_{N_\sigma}(C_\sigma(t))=\beta_\sigma(t,\rho_{N_\sigma}(C_\sigma(t))).$$
    43	Whenever this initial-value problem has a unique solution, write $\Phi_\sigma(t,s)c$ for its coefficient value at $t$ when its value at $s$ is $c$.
    44	
    45	A declared matching calculation supplies a finite list of renormalized amplitudes or Green-function values $A_{\sigma,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$, where $j$ indexes the selected external states and kinematics, $c$ is a coefficient vector, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}}$ denotes the common infrared regulator used in both descriptions. The matching map at scale $t$ is a supplied continuously differentiable map $M_t:U_+\to U_-$ between open coefficient domains. It is a matching map through the declared loop order, operator dimension, and mass-expansion order when, for every selected $j$ and every admitted $c\in U_+$, the prescribed truncations of $A_{+,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ and $A_{-,j}(t,M_t(c);\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ agree. The truncation projection and its retained orders are part of the datum. This equality records only the selected amplitudes and orders; it neither estimates discarded terms nor asserts that a matching calculation exists for every model.
    46	
    47	A matching map is RG-compatible on these domains when, in realified coefficient coordinates,
    48	$$\partial_t\bigl(\rho_{N_-}\circ M_t\circ\rho_{N_+}^{-1}\bigr)(x)+D_x\bigl(\rho_{N_-}\circ M_t\circ\rho_{N_+}^{-1}\bigr)(x)\,\beta_+(t,x)=\beta_-\!\left(t,\bigl(\rho_{N_-}\circ M_t\circ\rho_{N_+}^{-1}\bigr)(x)\right)$$
    49	for every admitted $(t,x)$. Here $D_x$ is the ordinary real derivative with respect to the coefficient coordinates. RG-compatibility is an additional condition to be checked from the supplied beta functions and matching calculation; it does not follow merely from defining $M_t$ by amplitude equality.
    50	
    51	For several thresholds, let $t_1>\cdots>t_k$ be their logarithmic scales, let $\Phi_j$ be the evolution in the theory active between thresholds $t_j$ and $t_{j+1}$, and let $M_j(t_j)$ map the coefficient data from the theory above threshold $j$ to the theory below it. The ordered high-to-low transport is
    52	$$\Phi_k(t_f,t_k)\circ M_k(t_k)\circ\Phi_{k-1}(t_k,t_{k-1})\circ\cdots\circ M_1(t_1)\circ\Phi_0(t_1,t_i),$$
    53	whenever all displayed flows and matching maps are defined. This definition specifies supplied finite-order data and their conventions; it computes no beta-function entry or matching coefficient and asserts no continuum limit.
