## Item checkpoint — ex-aqft-qed-threshold-matching

Authored a two-flavor QED worked example in the decoupling-subtraction scheme.
It takes Burgess's source-reported one-loop beta equations and continuous
muon-threshold boundary condition as model inputs, then integrates the
piecewise equations explicitly. The resulting inverse-coupling formulas are
given for m_e<Q<M_mu and Q>M_mu, with continuity checked at Q=M_mu and the
low-energy boundary recovered at Q=m_e. No measured coupling or mass value is
inserted, and higher-loop and higher-dimension corrections are not bounded.

Conventions: natural units; four-dimensional Minkowski signature
diag(-,+,+,+); a real photon field, the antisymmetric field strength, and
electron/muon Dirac fields of charge -e; alpha=e^2/(4 pi); logarithmic scale
u=log(mu^2/mu_*^2); source-specific DS matching continuous at M_mu. The proof
distinguishes the source-reported one-loop calculation from the conditional
integration of its beta equations.

Dependencies and roles:

- def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients — mathematical-premise.
- def-aqft-threshold-matching-map — formulation-prerequisite.

The item has dependency_level 5: one plus the maximum in-run supplier level,
4 from def-aqft-threshold-matching-map.

Source uses:
- Burgess full review, arXiv:hep-th/0701053, §4.1.4, equations (81)–(86):
  two-flavor model, active-flavor beta coefficients, DS threshold matching,
  boundary values, and piecewise running. This is the source of the adopted
  perturbative input.
- Manohar full lecture notes, arXiv:1804.05863, Chapter 7 §§7.1–7.2,
  equations (7.1)–(7.8): independent one-loop QED fermion contribution,
  mass-dependent decoupling and the MS comparison. This supports the
  threshold context; it does not replace the Burgess DS boundary conditions.

