 ## Statement
 
-Let $I=[t_i,t_f]$ be a compact interval contained in the common open logarithmic-scale domains of a finite-order high-energy EFT and its lower-energy EFT. Use the coefficient coordinates, realifications, RG vector fields $\beta_+$ and $\beta_-$, flow maps $\Phi_\pm$, and threshold matching map $M_t$ of the finite-order threshold-matching definition. Assume each beta field is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally uniformly in scale, and assume every flow used below exists on $I$. Assume $M_t$ is continuously differentiable and RG-compatible throughout the relevant domains. For an initial high-energy coefficient vector $c_i$ and any matching scale $s\in I$, define the low-energy vector at the fixed final scale by
-$$c_-^{(s)}(t_f):=\Phi_-\!\left(t_f,s\right)\!\left(M_s\!\left(\Phi_+(s,t_i)c_i\right)\right).$$
-Then $c_-^{(s)}(t_f)$ is independent of $s$. Consequently, each selected renormalized low-energy amplitude or Green-function value, evaluated at the same final scale, external states, kinematics, infrared regulator, and operator truncation, is independent of the intermediate matching scale through the retained orders. The matching definition makes that low-energy amplitude agree with its selected high-energy counterpart through those same orders. The same conclusion holds for any finite ordered sequence of thresholds when each matching map satisfies the stated compatibility condition and the required flows exist. This is a conditional deduction from the supplied RG and matching data; it neither calculates beta functions or matching coefficients nor bounds omitted orders or establishes a continuum QFT.
+Let $K=[t_i,t_f]$ be a compact interval contained in the common open logarithmic-scale domains of a finite-order high-energy EFT and its lower-energy EFT. Use the realified coefficient coordinates, RG vector fields $\beta_+$ and $\beta_-$, flow maps $\Phi_\pm$, and realified matching map $\widehat M_t$ of the finite-order threshold-matching definition. Assume each beta field is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally uniformly in scale, and assume every flow used below exists on $K$. Assume $\widehat M_t$ is continuously differentiable and RG-compatible throughout the relevant domains. For an initial high-energy realified coefficient vector $x_i$ and any matching scale $s\in K$, define the low-energy vector at the fixed final scale by
+$$x_-^{(s)}(t_f):=\Phi_-\!\left(t_f,s\right)\!\left(\widehat M_s\!\left(\Phi_+(s,t_i)x_i\right)\right).$$
+Then $x_-^{(s)}(t_f)$ is independent of $s$. Consequently, each selected renormalized low-energy amplitude or Green-function value, evaluated at the same final scale, external states, kinematics, infrared regulator, and operator truncation, is independent of the intermediate matching scale through the retained orders. The matching definition makes that low-energy amplitude agree with its selected high-energy counterpart through those same orders. The same conclusion holds for any finite ordered sequence of thresholds when each matching map satisfies the stated compatibility condition and the required flows exist. Contributions beyond the retained loop order, operator dimension, and mass expansion remain separate theoretical uncertainties; this result assigns them no numerical bounds. This is a conditional deduction from the supplied RG and matching data; it neither calculates beta functions or matching coefficients nor establishes a continuum QFT.
 
