items/def-aqft-threshold-matching-map.md:40:Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Fix a renormalization scale $\mu_0>0$ and a common open interval $J\subset(0,\infty)$ of renormalization scales $\mu$. Write $t=\log(\mu/\mu_0)$, so $t$ is dimensionless. A threshold datum consists of a high-energy description, denoted $+$, and a lower-energy effective description, denoted $-$. The descriptions have a common declared light-field sector and differ by the degrees of freedom retained across a specified threshold of mass $M>0$. The external-energy domain and the range of validity of the local expansion are supplied separately; in a heavy-particle expansion they ordinarily require external energy $E_{\mathrm{ext}}<M$. The Wilsonian matching scale $\mu_W$, the renormalization scale $\mu$, the threshold mass $M$, the external-energy bound $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, and the regulator cutoff $\Lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$ (denoted $\Lambda$ in the finite-regulator model data) have mass dimension one and are distinct data; no equality between $\mu_W$ and $\mu$ is part of this definition.
items/def-aqft-threshold-matching-map.md:42:For each sign $\sigma\in\{+,-\}$, supply a finite coefficient-coordinate space $\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ with $N_\sigma\ge1$, a declared ordered operator basis and power-counting truncation, and a renormalization-scheme coordinate map. The vector $C_\sigma(t)\in\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ is the column of dimensionless Wilson coefficients in that basis. The corresponding finite-order operator expression uses the supplied convention $C_{\sigma,i}O_{\sigma,i}/\Lambda_{\mathrm{op},\sigma}^{d_i-4}$, with each $O_{\sigma,i}$ of engineering dimension $d_i$ and $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op},\sigma}>0$ the coefficient-suppression scale for that theory, denoted $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}$ in its physical operator contract and $\Lambda$ in the generic scheme convention. It is distinct from $\Lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$, $M$, $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, $\mu_W$, and $\mu$ unless additional model data identify some of them. Write $\rho_{N_\sigma}$ for the realification that lists the real and imaginary parts of each coefficient. A supplied RG vector field is a continuous map $\beta_\sigma$ on an open subset of the realified scale-state space; an RG trajectory satisfies
items/def-aqft-threshold-matching-map.md:47:A declared matching calculation supplies a finite list of renormalized amplitudes or Green-function values $A_{\sigma,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$, where $j$ indexes the selected external states and kinematics, $c$ is a coefficient vector, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}}$ denotes the common infrared regulator used in both descriptions. Let $U_\sigma\subset\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ be open coefficient domains for $\sigma\in\{+,-\}$. The supplied matching family is a jointly continuously differentiable real map $\widehat{\mathcal M}:\mathcal U\to\rho_{N_-}(U_-)$ on an open domain $\mathcal U\subset J\times\rho_{N_+}(U_+)$. It defines $M_t(c):=\rho_{N_-}^{-1}(\widehat{\mathcal M}(t,\rho_{N_+}(c)))$ for each admitted $(t,c)$, and $\widehat M_t(x):=\widehat{\mathcal M}(t,x)$ on the corresponding slice. Thus the scale derivative and coefficient derivative in the compatibility condition below both exist and are continuous. It is a matching map through the declared loop order, operator dimension, and mass-expansion order when, for every selected $j$ and every admitted $c\in U_+$, the prescribed truncations of $A_{+,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ and $A_{-,j}(t,M_t(c);\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ agree. The truncation projection and its retained orders are part of the datum. This equality records only the selected amplitudes and orders; it neither estimates discarded terms nor asserts that a matching calculation exists for every model.
