items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:41:Let $K=[t_i,t_f]$ be a compact interval contained in the common open logarithmic-scale domains of a finite-order high-energy EFT and its lower-energy EFT. Use the realified coefficient coordinates, RG vector fields $\beta_+$ and $\beta_-$, flow maps $\Phi_\pm$, and realified matching map $\widehat M_t$ of the finite-order threshold-matching definition. Assume each beta field is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally uniformly in scale, and assume every flow used below exists on $K$. Assume $\widehat M_t$ is continuously differentiable and RG-compatible throughout the relevant domains. For an initial high-energy realified coefficient vector $x_i$ and any matching scale $s\in K$, define the low-energy vector at the fixed final scale by
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:57:## Facts & Assumptions
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:63:[F2] [[def-aqft-threshold-matching-map]]: the realified matching map $\widehat M_t$ is continuously differentiable between the high- and low-energy real coefficient domains; RG compatibility means $\partial_t\widehat M_t(x)+D_x\widehat M_t(x)\beta_+(t,x)=\beta_-(t,\widehat M_t(x))$. Its selected high- and low-energy amplitudes are matched with the same infrared regulator through the declared truncation.
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:79:1.1 Let $x_+(t)=\Phi_+(t,t_i)x_i$ and set $y(t)=\widehat M_t(x_+(t))$. The chain rule and [A2] give $y'(t)=\partial_t\widehat M_t(x_+(t))+D_x\widehat M_t(x_+(t))\,\beta_+(t,x_+(t))=\beta_-(t,y(t))$ for every $t\in K$. Thus $y$ is a low-energy RG trajectory on the whole common interval. [given, F1, F2, A1, A2, algebra]
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:81:2.1 Fix any $s\in K$. The low-energy trajectory obtained by matching at $s$ starts at $\widehat M_s(x_+(s))=y(s)$ and solves the low-energy RG equation. Since $y$ solves that same initial-value problem, [F3] implies that the two solutions agree on their common interval $K$. Therefore $x_-^{(s)}(t_f)=y(t_f)$, which does not depend on $s$. [F2, F3, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:83:3.1 At the fixed final scale, the selected amplitude or Green-function value is evaluated from the same low-energy coefficient vector $y(t_f)$ with the same external states, kinematics, regulator, and truncation [A3]. It is therefore identical for every matching scale $s$. By the matching condition in [F2], this is the corresponding high-energy prediction through the retained orders. [F2, A3, step 2.1, algebra]
items/pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence.md:85:4.1 For finitely many thresholds, apply steps 1.1–3.1 to the first matching map, then to each subsequent map in the ordered composition. Each compatible map transports its incoming trajectory to a solution of the next theory's RG equation, and uniqueness identifies that solution independently of the selected matching scale. Finite induction gives the same final coefficient vector and selected prediction for every compatible choice of intermediate scales. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, induction] ∎
