Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Fix a renormalization scale $\mu_0>0$ and a common open interval $J\subset(0,\infty)$ of renormalization scales $\mu$. Write $t=\log(\mu/\mu_0)$, so $t$ is dimensionless. A threshold datum consists of a high-energy description, denoted $+$, and a lower-energy effective description, denoted $-$. The descriptions have a common declared light-field sector and differ by the degrees of freedom retained across a specified threshold of mass $M>0$. The external-energy domain and the range of validity of the local expansion are supplied separately; in a heavy-particle expansion they ordinarily require external energy $E_{\mathrm{ext}}<M$. The Wilsonian matching scale $\mu_W$, the renormalization scale $\mu$, the threshold mass $M$, the external-energy bound $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, and the regulator cutoff $\Lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$ (denoted $\Lambda$ in the finite-regulator model data) have mass dimension one and are distinct data; no equality between $\mu_W$ and $\mu$ is part of this definition.

For each sign $\sigma\in\{+,-\}$, supply a finite coefficient-coordinate space $\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ with $N_\sigma\ge1$, a declared ordered operator basis and power-counting truncation, and a renormalization-scheme coordinate map. The vector $C_\sigma(t)\in\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ is the column of dimensionless Wilson coefficients in that basis. The corresponding finite-order operator expression uses the supplied convention $C_{\sigma,i}O_{\sigma,i}/\Lambda_{\mathrm{op},\sigma}^{d_i-4}$, with each $O_{\sigma,i}$ of engineering dimension $d_i$ and $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op},\sigma}>0$ the coefficient-suppression scale for that theory, denoted $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}$ in its physical operator contract and $\Lambda$ in the generic scheme convention. It is distinct from $\Lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$, $M$, $E_{\mathrm{ext}}$, $\mu_W$, and $\mu$ unless additional model data identify some of them. Write $\rho_{N_\sigma}$ for the realification that lists the real and imaginary parts of each coefficient. A supplied RG vector field is a continuous map $\beta_\sigma:\Omega_\sigma\to\mathbb R^{2N_\sigma$ on a supplied open domain $\Omega_\sigma\subset J\times\mathbb R^{2N_\sigma}$; an RG trajectory satisfies
$$\frac{d}{dt}\rho_{N_\sigma}(C_\sigma(t))=\beta_\sigma(t,\rho_{N_\sigma}(C_\sigma(t))).$$
For each sign, the physical operator datum is an instance of the selected physical EFT operator contract: it specifies the finite field species and jet generators, their graded field spaces, domains, regularity, reality data, and engineering weights; the Lorentz, gauge, flavor, and spurion representations; and the finite derivative, field-degree, dimension, and loop cutoffs. It also declares the relation subspace and representatives, and either verifies on each retained jet space that the chosen algebraic invariants are the intended physical invariants or labels that quotient as a formal algebraic selection only. If a non-Abelian gauge sector is selected, its actual compact real global group, bundle, sectors, unitary representations, and smooth connection are part of the data. Each finite basis represents only this supplied fixed-order quotient; no completeness claim about all physical relations or an infinite operator tower is made. These field and counting inputs are not inferred from coefficient coordinates.
For $x\in\mathbb R^{2N_\sigma}$, write $\Phi_\sigma(t,s)x\in\mathbb R^{2N_\sigma}$ for the value at $t$ of the unique realified coefficient solution with value $x$ at $s$, whenever that solution exists on the interval between $s$ and $t$.

A declared matching calculation supplies a finite list of renormalized amplitudes or Green-function values $A_{\sigma,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$, where $j$ indexes the selected external states and kinematics, $c$ is a coefficient vector, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}}$ denotes the common infrared regulator used in both descriptions. Let $U_\sigma\subset\mathbb C^{N_\sigma}$ be open coefficient domains for $\sigma\in\{+,-\}$. The supplied matching family is a jointly continuously differentiable real map $\widehat{\mathcal M}:\mathcal U\to\rho_{N_-}(U_-)$ on an open domain $\mathcal U\subset\{(t,x)\in J\times\rho_{N_+}(U_+):(t,x)\in\Omega_+,\ (t,\widehat{\mathcal M}(t,x))\in\Omega_-\}$. It defines $M_t(c):=\rho_{N_-}^{-1}(\widehat{\mathcal M}(t,\rho_{N_+}(c)))$ for each admitted $(t,c)$, and $\widehat M_t(x):=\widehat{\mathcal M}(t,x)$ on the corresponding slice. Thus the scale derivative and coefficient derivative in the compatibility condition below both exist and are continuous. It is a matching map through the declared loop order, operator dimension, and mass-expansion order when, for every selected $j$ and every admitted $c\in U_+$, the prescribed truncations of $A_{+,j}(t,c;\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ and $A_{-,j}(t,M_t(c);\lambda_{\mathrm{IR}})$ agree. The truncation projection and its retained orders are part of the datum. This equality records only the selected amplitudes and orders; it neither estimates discarded terms nor asserts that a matching calculation exists for every model.

A matching map is RG-compatible on these domains when, in realified coefficient coordinates,
$$\partial_t\widehat M_t(x)+D_x\widehat M_t(x)\,\beta_+(t,x)=\beta_-\!\left(t,\widehat M_t(x)\right)$$
for every admitted $(t,x)$. Here $D_x$ is the ordinary real derivative with respect to the coefficient coordinates. RG-compatibility is an additional condition to be checked from the supplied beta functions and matching calculation; it does not follow merely from defining $M_t$ by amplitude equality.

For several thresholds, let $t_1>\cdots>t_k$ be their logarithmic scales, let $\Phi_j$ be the realified evolution in the theory active between thresholds $t_j$ and $t_{j+1}$, and let $\widehat M_j(t_j)$ map realified coefficient data from the theory above threshold $j$ to the theory below it. The ordered high-to-low transport is
